<DocsLayout title="Architecture" eyebrow="Trust boundaries" intro="A deliberately small public service in front of one private, durable persistence engine.">
  <div class="doc-architecture"><div><small>Public</small><strong>Application</strong><span>WebAuthn + cookie</span></div><b>→</b><div class="accent"><small>Authentication</small><strong>RustyAuth</strong><span>Rust + Axum</span></div><b>→</b><div class="dark"><small>Private</small><strong>SableDB</strong><span>Valkey protocol</span></div></div>
  <h2>Why Rust</h2><p>Rust gives the public authentication boundary memory safety without a garbage collector, predictable resource use, and a single deployable binary. Axum keeps the HTTP surface explicit and composable.</p>
  <h2>Why SableDB</h2><p>SableDB is a Rust-native, Valkey-compatible persistence engine. RustyAuth uses atomic pipelines for multi-key identity operations and requires a private, volume-backed deployment. It is the only online durable store.</p>
  <h2>Typed service contracts</h2><p>The versioned <code>rustyauth.events.v1</code> contract streams ordered events, while <code>rustyauth.identity.v1</code> exposes safe account reads, exact search and controlled metadata mutations. Both run over Connect, gRPC-Web or native gRPC with separately scoped bearers. Browser-facing WebAuthn ceremonies remain HTTP-oriented.</p>
  <h2>Responsibility split</h2><ul><li>RustyAuth proves identity and manages credentials, ceremonies, sessions and authentication claims.</li><li>Your application owns authorization, roles, entitlements and resource ownership.</li><li>SableDB is never exposed as the public authentication interface.</li></ul>
</DocsLayout>
